<!--
  Allow the `default` user to connect from the Docker bridge GATEWAY ADDRESS
  ONLY (172.17.0.1/32).

  The stock clickhouse-server image ships
  /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/default-user.xml, which restricts `default` to
  ::1 and 127.0.0.1 ("available only locally"). That check is applied to the
  source address AS THE SERVER SEES IT, and a host process reaching the
  container through `-p 127.0.0.1:8123:8123` is NAT'd, so it arrives from the
  bridge gateway rather than loopback and is refused.

  Confirmed from the server log rather than assumed:

    Access(user directories): from: 172.17.0.1, user: default:
    Authentication failed: Code: 195. DB::Exception:
    Connections from 172.17.0.1 are not allowed. (IP_ADDRESS_NOT_ALLOWED)

  Note that the client is told something different: code 194,
  "Authentication failed: password is incorrect, or there is no user with such
  name" (REQUIRED_PASSWORD). ClickHouse masks IP_ADDRESS_NOT_ALLOWED on the way
  out so as not to leak whether a user exists or which networks are permitted.
  The message therefore points squarely at credentials when the actual cause is
  the source address — worth knowing before chasing a password that was never
  wrong.

  WHY /32 AND NOT THE SUBNET: 172.17.0.0/16 (or /12) would grant passwordless
  `default` access to EVERY container on the default bridge — ray-worker and
  any other stack that happens to be up — not just the host. Host-NAT'd traffic
  arrives from the gateway address specifically, and 172.17.0.1 is the only
  172.x address appearing anywhere in the server log, so a /32 is exact rather
  than merely tighter. A container would have to impersonate the gateway to
  benefit from this grant.

  This file only widens the allowed SOURCE RANGE. It sets no password and
  grants no new privileges. Exposure is unchanged in practice because the host
  port binding is already loopback-only (127.0.0.1:8123 / 127.0.0.1:9000), so
  the container is unreachable from off the machine regardless.

  Named zz-* so it merges AFTER default-user.xml (users.d is applied in lexical
  order). Requires an explicit reload — ClickHouse did not pick up the change
  on its own:

    docker cp bench/ch-allow-docker-bridge.xml \
      meshroad-ch:/etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/zz-allow-docker-bridge.xml
    docker exec meshroad-ch clickhouse-client —query "SYSTEM RELOAD CONFIG"

  NOTE: this lives in the container filesystem, not in the meshroad-ch-data
  volume, so it must be re-applied if the container is recreated. If the bridge
  gateway ever differs (a non-default network, or a recreated bridge), re-read
  it from `docker inspect meshroad-ch —format '{{range .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{.Gateway}}{{end}}'`
  rather than widening this range.
-->
<clickhouse>
  <users>
    <default>
      <networks>
        <ip>::1</ip>
        <ip>127.0.0.1</ip>
        <ip>172.17.0.1/32</ip>
      </networks>
    </default>
  </users>
</clickhouse>
